{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-18322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(1,0,-2)$, $B(1,2,4)$, and $C(3,-2,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-18322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,2,4⟩,\\,c=⟨3,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨1,2,4⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{40}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{170}})≈{58}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-18322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60cd1315cf2b6b75f4b50b0389fd8558a2160d0ff8f42305f4c35d7522a171fd"} {"question" "q-18322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-18322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{85}^{∘}"] ["math" "{113}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{58}^{∘}"] ["math" "{70}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-18322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{58}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60cd1315cf2b6b75f4b50b0389fd8558a2160d0ff8f42305f4c35d7522a171fd"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{120}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{75}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-19110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{90}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the points $A(5,3t+2,2),$ $B(1,3t,2)$ and $C(1,4t,3).$ Find $∠ABC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩,\\,b=⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩,\\,c=⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠ABC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,3t+2,\\,2⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3t,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}=4$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =4 \\\\ 4\\cdot 0+2\\cdot t+0\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 0+2t+0 & =4 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =⟨4,2,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})≈{66.42}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60cd1315cf2b6b75f4b50b0389fd8558a2160d0ff8f42305f4c35d7522a171fd"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{53.13}^{∘}"] ["math" "{64.52}^{∘}"] ["math" "{66.42}^{∘}"] ["math" "{78.46}^{∘}"] ["math" "{84.26}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-77165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{66.42}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the points $A(2t,-1,5)$, $B(-3,t,1)$ and $C(2t+1,2,-t).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60cd1315cf2b6b75f4b50b0389fd8558a2160d0ff8f42305f4c35d7522a171fd"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{71.36}^{∘}"] ["math" "{73.42}^{∘}"] ["math" "{75.63}^{∘}"] ["math" "{77.21}^{∘}"] ["math" "{79.84}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-101517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{75.63}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,2)$, $B(3,-5,1)$, and $C(1,-4,-1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,2⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,c=⟨1,-4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}})≈{41}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "60cd1315cf2b6b75f4b50b0389fd8558a2160d0ff8f42305f4c35d7522a171fd"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{35}^{∘}"] ["math" "{38}^{∘}"] ["math" "{41}^{∘}"] ["math" "{46}^{∘}"] ["math" "{50}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"} {"question" "q-124813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{41}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/state.json" "file_sha256" "df20584da19b2f9f4eeaa063ef843a7de3d64c2eb29336c787e050a979074d28"}] "usage" {"q-19110" []} "basis" 842 "retractions" [[17592186106032 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0, 4, 5)$, $Q(1, 5, 4)$, and $